{%- comment -%}
  The first-order welcome discount, in one place.

  A theme cannot create a discount — only Shopify can. The code named in
  Settings › Welcome offer has to exist in admin › Discounts as a percentage
  off the order, limited to one use per customer, above the minimum purchase
  amount named in the same settings. This snippet is everything the storefront
  can legitimately do about it: say whether it can still be claimed, put it on
  the bag, say how far a bag is from earning it, and confirm once it is on.
  Before this existed the band promised 10% and nothing anywhere ever applied
  anything.

  The minimum is the one number that has to be kept in step by hand. Shopify
  enforces what the discount says; the storefront only repeats it, so a
  settings value that disagrees with admin turns into a promise checkout will
  refuse.

  Claiming is a real navigation to /discount/CODE, not a background fetch.
  That URL is the documented way to attach a code to a cart and the only one
  Shopify guarantees; it hands the shopper straight back to `redirect`, so the
  trip costs a reload and nothing else.

  Params
    style  'cta'  gold button — the membership band
           'row'  compact strip — cart page, cart drawer, account page
    auto   true   claim it without being asked, once per browser. Only the
                  account page passes this: it is where sign-up lands, and
                  where "applied automatically" has to come true.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign wo_code = settings.welcome_offer_code | default: '' | strip | upcase
  assign wo_pct = settings.welcome_offer_percent | default: '10' | strip
  assign wo_style = style | default: 'row'

  # Cart-level applications carry the code as their title, so this is what
  # "already on the bag" looks like from Liquid. Titles come back in whatever
  # case the merchant typed, hence the upcase on both sides.
  assign wo_applied = false
  for wo_d in cart.cart_level_discount_applications
    assign wo_title = wo_d.title | upcase
    if wo_title == wo_code
      assign wo_applied = true
    endif
  endfor

  # One order is all it takes to spend a first-order discount. A guest is
  # eligible in the sense that joining is what claims it.
  assign wo_spent = false
  if customer and customer.orders_count > 0
    assign wo_spent = true
  endif

  # Theme settings › Promotions & cashback is the switch over every offer on
  # the storefront at once, so an exclusivity commitment to a cashback partner
  # is one checkbox rather than a hunt through the surfaces that advertise a
  # discount. Checked first because nothing below it matters if we are not
  # running promotions at all.
  assign wo_on = false
  if settings.promos_enabled and settings.welcome_offer_enabled and wo_code != blank and wo_spent == false
    assign wo_on = true
  endif

  # The discount carries a minimum purchase amount, so the storefront has to
  # as well: a code that silently declines under AED 150 reads as a broken
  # code. Stated in whole currency units in settings, held in cents here
  # because every price on the cart is.
  assign wo_min = settings.welcome_offer_min | default: '' | strip
  assign wo_min_cents = 0
  if wo_min != blank
    assign wo_min_cents = wo_min | times: 100
  endif

  # Measured against the price before discounts, which is what Shopify's
  # minimum is tested against — comparing the discounted total would let a bag
  # drop back under the floor the moment the discount came off it.
  assign wo_short = 0
  if wo_min_cents > 0 and cart.original_total_price < wo_min_cents
    assign wo_short = wo_min_cents | minus: cart.original_total_price
  endif

  # Come back to the page the shopper was on. request.path drops the query
  # string, which is what we want — no filters or pagination to re-encode.
  assign wo_back = request.path | default: '/'
-%}

{%- if wo_on -%}
  {%- capture wo_apply_url -%}/discount/{{ wo_code | url_encode }}?redirect={{ wo_back | url_encode }}{%- endcapture -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Truthiness, not `customer == blank`: a nil customer is only reliably
    testable this way, and an equality test that quietly reads false hands the
    signed-in apply button to logged-out visitors.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if wo_style == 'cta' -%}
    {%- if customer -%}
      {%- if wo_applied -%}
        <span class="wo-active"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">check_circle</span>Your {{ wo_pct }}% welcome discount is on the bag — <b>{{ wo_code }}</b>{% if wo_min_cents > 0 %}, from {{ wo_min_cents | money_with_currency }}{% endif %}</span>
        <a class="btn-o" href="{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">storefront</span>Start shopping</a>
      {%- else -%}
        <a class="btn-g" href="{{ wo_apply_url | strip }}"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">redeem</span>Apply my {{ wo_pct }}% discount</a>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- else -%}
      <a class="btn-g" href="{{ routes.account_register_url }}"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">person_add</span>Claim {{ wo_pct }}% Discount</a>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The compact strip is for signed-in customers only. A guest standing at the
    cart is being asked to open an account mid-checkout, which is a worse trade
    than the band above the footer already offers them.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- elsif customer -%}
    {%- if wo_applied -%}
      {%- if wo_short > 0 -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          On the bag but not yet earning anything. Saying "applied" and showing
          no money off is the version of this that gets support tickets.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="wo-row is-wait">
          <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">schedule</span>
          <span><b>{{ wo_code }}</b> is on your bag — {{ wo_pct }}% comes off at {{ wo_min_cents | money_with_currency }}. Add <b>{{ wo_short | money_with_currency }}</b> more.</span>
        </div>
      {%- else -%}
        <div class="wo-row is-on">
          <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">check_circle</span>
          <span>Welcome discount <b>{{ wo_code }}</b> applied — {{ wo_pct }}% off this order.</span>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- else -%}
      <a class="wo-row" href="{{ wo_apply_url | strip }}">
        <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">redeem</span>
        <span>You have <b>{{ wo_pct }}% off</b> your first order{% if wo_min_cents > 0 %} over {{ wo_min_cents | money_with_currency }}{% endif %} — apply it</span>
        <span class="mi wo-go" aria-hidden="true">arrow_forward</span>
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if auto and customer and wo_applied == false -%}
    <script>
      /* Straight after sign-up, this is the redirect that makes "applied
         automatically" true. The flag is written before the trip, not after:
         a code that has been deleted, expired or spent redirects back having
         applied nothing, and a flag set afterwards would send the account
         page round that loop on every load. Once set, the visible Apply
         button above is the way back in. */
      (function () {
        var key = 'tfs_welcome_{{ wo_code | escape }}_{{ customer.id }}';
        try {
          if (localStorage.getItem(key)) return;
          localStorage.setItem(key, '1');
        } catch (e) { return; }
        window.location.replace({{ wo_apply_url | strip | json }});
      })();
    </script>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
